# PART 5 — MOTION QUALITY AND PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS

The words "buttery smooth" must translate into measurable engineering decisions.

Establish and enforce these principles:

### Animation performance
- Prefer transform and opacity animations when possible.
- Avoid unnecessarily animating expensive layout properties.
- Avoid excessive backdrop-filter recalculation.
- Avoid unnecessary JavaScript-driven animation loops.
- Use GPU-accelerated techniques appropriately.
- Avoid creating unnecessary rendering layers.
- Keep animation code isolated and reusable.
- Cancel or clean up animations when components unmount.
- Avoid memory leaks.
- Avoid conflicting animation systems.
- Coordinate animations with the rendering engine.

### Timing and easing

Establish a coherent motion language.

Use short, responsive transitions for controls and longer, smoother transitions for major navigation and spatial movements.

Choose easing based on the movement:
- Ease-out for entering elements.
- Ease-in for exiting elements.
- Ease-in-out for state changes and spatial movement.
- Spring-like movement only where it feels appropriate.
- Linear movement for continuous processes that should represent uniform progression.

Treat the following as starting points, not universal fixed values:

- Micro-interactions: approximately 100–180 ms.
- Button and control feedback: approximately 120–220 ms.
- Dropdowns and popovers: approximately 160–240 ms.
- Panel transitions: approximately 200–350 ms.
- Major navigation transitions: approximately 250–450 ms.
- Cinematic geographic transitions: determined by distance, geometry, loading and user interaction.

The animation must not feel slow merely because a longer duration has been chosen.

### Frame-rate targets

Aim for:
- 60 FPS on supported standard displays.
- 120 FPS where hardware and display support make it practical.
- No avoidable frame drops during ordinary UI interactions.
- Graceful quality adaptation on lower-performance devices.

Measure actual frame behaviour instead of claiming these targets were achieved without testing.

### Accessibility

Respect the operating system's reduced-motion preference.

Provide an appropriate reduced-motion experience:
- Remove unnecessary decorative movement.
- Reduce large camera and panel movements.
- Preserve essential interaction feedback.
- Avoid flashing and disorienting effects.

Animation must never be required to understand the interface.

